  data:  # caa4
    kind: invariant  # caa4
    title: "Python analyzer drops nested function definitions and bare-name calls to them (~121 missing Symbols, ~360 missing call edges on self-analysis)"  # caa4
    status: violated  # caa4
    priority: 2  # caa4
    parent:  # caa4
    tags:  # caa4
    - analyzer  # caa4
    - python  # caa4
    - call_graph  # caa4
    - completeness  # caa4
    - self_analysis  # caa4
    - dogfooding  # caa4
    isbefore: []  # caa4
    duplicate_of: []  # caa4
    not_duplicate_of: []  # caa4
    pr_ref:  # caa4
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 10).**\n\n**Symptom.** On hypergumbo's self-analysis, the Python analyzer fails to capture **nested function definitions** as Symbols AND fails to capture **bare-name calls** to those nested functions as call edges.\n\n**Concrete case.** `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/sketch.py:2991-3229` defines `_collect_important_files`, which contains 4 inner functions (verified by AST):\n\n```python\ndef _collect_important_files(...) -> list[str]:\n    ...\n    def to_relative(path: str) -> str:       # line 3036\n        ...\n    def get_root_dir(path: str) -> str:       # line 3047\n        ...\n    def is_root_level_file(path: str) -> bool: # line 3052\n        ...\n    def add_file(path: str) -> bool:           # line 3056\n        ...\n    # ~12 call sites of these inner functions follow, e.g.:\n    #   path = to_relative(path)              line 3064\n    #   root = get_root_dir(path)             line 3070\n    #   if is_root_level_file(path):          line 3075\n    #   add_file(pattern)                     line 3103\n    #   add_file(test_files[0])               line 3122\n    #   add_file(sym.path)                    line 3131\n```\n\nThe self-analysis baseline reports:\n\n```python\n# Symbols matching the 4 inner-function names:\n$ grep ... | python\nto_relative:        0 Symbols\nget_root_dir:       0 Symbols\nis_root_level_file: 0 Symbols\nadd_file:           0 Symbols\n```\n\nAnd the 16 outbound `calls` edges from `_collect_important_files` (its `src` ID) point exclusively at stdlib attribute methods: `Path`, `append`, `get`, `add`, `exists`, `items`, `lower`, `relative_to`, `is_file`, `update`, `sort`, `iterdir`, `is_absolute`, `resolve`, `fnmatch`, `SOURCE_EXTENSIONS.values`. **None** of the ~12 calls to `to_relative` / `get_root_dir` / `is_root_level_file` / `add_file` are captured.\n\n**Scale of the gap.** AST scan of production code (`packages/*/src/`, excluding tests):\n\n| Metric | Count |\n|---|---:|\n| `.py` files scanned | 294 |\n| Total function defs | 3,907 |\n| **Nested function defs (the analyzer's blind spot)** | **121** |\n| Nested as % of total | 3.1% |\n\nAssuming each nested function is called ~3 times on average inside its enclosing function, that's ~360 missing call edges from production Python alone — and these are exactly the calls that structurally matter most (helpers that organize the logic of a longer function are higher signal than the stdlib calls that DO get captured).\n\n**Invariant statement.** Every function definition emitted by the AST as a `FunctionDef` or `AsyncFunctionDef` node — at any nesting depth — must be emitted as a Symbol by the Python analyzer. Every call to a bare name that resolves to a nested function defined in an enclosing scope must be emitted as a `calls` edge with that nested function's Symbol as the `dst`.\n\n**Severity.** Medium-high. Three consumer-visible failures:\n\n1. **`hypergumbo explain` misrepresents what a function \"calls.\"** On self-analysis, asking for `_collect_important_files`'s callees returns 16 stdlib methods and zero internal helpers. The structural understanding of the function (organized into 4 inner helpers + a driver) is invisible.\n\n2. **`hypergumbo slice` skips control flow that's literally in the function body.** Forward slicing from `_collect_important_files` returns 17 nodes (entry + 16 stdlib externals). The \"real\" subgraph that an agent would want to read — the 4 inner helpers and their relationships — is dropped.\n\n3. **Centrality / dead-code estimates are skewed.** A function with many internal helpers gets undercounted on out-degree; the helpers get counted as 0-in-degree dead code (if they were emitted at all) or are simply invisible (current state).\n\n**Root cause (hypothesis).** Looking at `packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py.py`, the AST visitor likely uses a top-level traversal pattern that emits Symbols only for module-level and class-level `FunctionDef` / `AsyncFunctionDef` nodes, and doesn't recurse into function bodies looking for nested `FunctionDef`. Independently, the call-resolver probably scans `Call` nodes but only matches bare names against module-scope names (functions, imports), with no awareness that the enclosing function's scope contains additional names.\n\n**Fix proposal.**\n\n1. **Emit nested functions as Symbols.** Walk the entire AST, not just top-level/class-level. Use a fully-qualified name like `_collect_important_files.add_file` to distinguish from same-name nested functions in other parents. Mark with a `kind` that signals nesting (either `kind=\"function\"` + a `meta.nesting_parent` field, or a new `kind=\"nested_function\"` — recommend the former to avoid axis expansion).\n\n2. **Resolve bare-name calls against the enclosing scope.** When resolving a bare-name `Call`, walk up the enclosing function scopes (then class scope, then module scope) before falling through to external. If a nested function with that name exists in any enclosing scope, emit a `calls` edge to it.\n\n**Test gap.** Property test on the self-analysis fixture: assert that for every `Call(func=Name(id=N))` AST node inside a function body, either (a) a `calls` edge with that Symbol resolution exists, or (b) `N` is recorded as unresolved/external in the edge metadata. Currently the 4 inner-function calls in `_collect_important_files` violate both branches silently.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py.py` — Python analyzer (likely needs the FunctionDef visitor change)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/sketch.py:2991-3229` — the concrete case used to validate\n- `INV-fusus-...` (filed round 4) — stable_id collisions; would be partly mitigated if nested functions had their own Symbols\n- `INV-sopon-...` (filed round 2) — sibling Symbol-emission completeness gap on the linker side\n- `WI-luvig-...` (filed round 7) — `canonical_name` would be useful here for `_collect_important_files.add_file` style fully-qualified naming"  # caa4
    fields:  # caa4
      statement: "Every function definition emitted by the AST as a FunctionDef or AsyncFunctionDef node, at any nesting depth, must be emitted as a Symbol by the Python analyzer. Every call to a bare name that resolves to a nested function defined in an enclosing scope must be emitted as a 'calls' edge with that nested function's Symbol as the dst."  # caa4
      root_cause: "py.py AST visitor emits Symbols only for module-level and class-level FunctionDef nodes; doesn't recurse into function bodies for nested FunctionDef. Independently, the call-resolver matches bare names against module-scope only, no awareness of enclosing-function scope. Concrete validation case: _collect_important_files in sketch.py:2991-3229 defines 4 inner functions and calls them ~12 times; 0 of those inner functions are Symbols and 0 of those calls are edges on self-analysis baseline."  # caa4
